</ol>
<h2 id="how-to-send-a-message-using-whatsapp-business-api"><b>How to Send a Message using WhatsApp Business API?</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">There are two different ways to send messages via WhatsApp API. Here’s how you can do it with two different methods:</span></p>
<h3 id="1-using-metas-cloud-api-technical-path"><b>1. Using Meta&#8217;s Cloud API (Technical Path)</b></h3>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">If you have developer support, you can send WhatsApp API messages directly through Meta’s Cloud API. Here are simple steps that you need to follow:</span></p>
<p><b>Step 1: Create a Meta Developer Account: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Visit the </span><a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" href="https://developers.facebook.com/"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Meta for Developers</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;"> portal and create your Facebook account if you don’t have one.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 2: Select an App:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> Now tap on “Create a new app” and select “Business” as your app type to access the WhatsApp Cloud API.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 3: Enter your basic information:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> Fill in your basic business information. If you already have a Facebook Business Manager account, pick it from the dropdown menu. Otherwise, select “No Business Manager Account Selected,” and Meta will automatically create one. Tap “Create App”.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 4: Add the WhatsApp Product: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Now you are signed up into your Meta dashboard successfully. From here, go to the “WhatsApp” icon and tap on the “Set up” button under it.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 5: Select a Meta Business Account: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Accept the Terms and Conditions. Then, Meta will automatically provide you with a temporary test phone number along with a Phone Number ID and a WhatsApp Business Account ID.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 6: Verify a Recipient Number: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Navigate to WhatsApp &gt; API Setup and add a phone number that you want to test with. Verify it using the verification code sent to your WhatsApp account.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 7: Generate an Access Token: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">On the same API setup page, tap on Generate access token.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 8: Create and Approve Message Templates:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> If you want to send messages outside the 24-hour window, create a message template and submit it for Meta approval.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 9: Send Your First WhatsApp Message:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> Use Meta’s API endpoint to send a message. Use the pre-approved template and send it via Postman, cURL, or your application.</span></p>
<p><b>Step 10: Monitor Message Delivery &amp; Scale with Automation:</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> Track key metrics like message status, delivery reports, and customer engagement from the Meta dashboard. You can even connect the API to your CRM, E-commerce tool, and chatbot to automate support and marketing.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">That’s all. But if you want to avoid this tech-heavy process and handle API setup, webhooks, and template management yourself, you can access it through the </span><a href="/resources/blog/whatsapp-api-providers/"><span style="font-weight: 400;">best WhatsApp API providers</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;">. </span></p>
